explanations.E0034:
# E0034: name defined twice

Functions, classes, enums, interfaces, constants and type aliases share one namespace. The fields and methods of a class share one, and so do the variants and methods of an enum. The labels of one variant's fields share one of their own, so two variants can use the same label. Each name is defined once in its namespace. The prelude functions `print`, `eprint`, `panic`, `read_line`, `dbg` and `exchange` are built in, so no top-level item can take their names.

The requirements of an interface and of the interfaces it inherits share one namespace too. An interface that declares a requirement again, when an interface it inherits has the same name, is reported: the inherited interface already requires it, and the secondary label points to that requirement.

The associated types of an interface and of the interfaces it inherits share one namespace too, and so do the choices of one class or `extend` block. An interface that declares `type Item` again, when an interface it inherits has the same name, is reported.

The methods of a class or an enum, and the methods of its plain `extend` blocks (`extend Circle { ... }`), share one namespace with its fields or variants. The methods of an `extend Circle: Shape` block belong to that conformance, so they can share a name with a method of the class.

Two plain blocks clash when one type fits both targets. `extend<T> Stack<T>` and `extend Stack<i64>` both give `Stack<i64>` a method `top`, and the error points at the second. `extend Stack<i64>` and `extend Stack<f64>` do not clash, so each can have a method `sum`.

## Why

A call or a type that names a duplicate could mean either definition. Sisu keeps the first and reports the second.

## Broken

```sisu
fn double(n: i64) -> i64 {
    n * 2
}

fn double(n: i64) -> i64 {
    n + n
}

fn main() {
    print(double(2))
}
```

## Fixed

```sisu
fn double(n: i64) -> i64 {
    n * 2
}

fn main() {
    print(double(2))
}
```

explanations.E0063:
# E0063: field or method that does not exist

A field read or a method call must name a member of the receiver's type: a field or method of its class, the array methods `len()`, `sum()` and `contains(x)`, the `string` methods `len()`, `chars()`, `bytes()` and `words()`, or the methods of `f64` and `i64`, such as `x.sqrt()` and `n.max(m)`. A built-in type's name takes a constant: `f64.pi`, `f64.tau`, `f64.e`, `f64.infinity`, `f64.nan`, `f64.max`, `f64.min_positive` and `f64.epsilon`. No other type has any, so `i64.max` is this error too. A method of `f64` is no method of `i64`: `n.sqrt()` with an `i64` `n` is this error too. A constructor's labels must name fields of its class, or of the labeled payload of its variant. The methods of an enum count as methods of its type, but its value has no fields: that is [E0121](E0121.md).

When a member of the receiver's type has a similar name, the error offers it as an unsafe fix. The unsafe fix may be wrong: the name could be a member that was never written. For `let x = 2.5` and `x.sqrtt()`, the unsafe fix is `sqrt`; for `f64.PI`, it is `pi`.

The same error is reported for a method call on a value of a type parameter: the type parameter has only the methods of its bounds. When exactly one interface of the program declares a method of that name, and it takes no type parameters, the help adds the bound: for `x.area()` on a value of type `T`, `add a bound: T: Shape`. If `T` has bounds already, the help lists them with the new one.

## Why

Sisu does not resolve a misspelled member name on its own. A misspelled name would otherwise read some other value or run some other code.

## Broken

```sisu
class Point {
    x: i64
}

fn main() {
    let p = Point(x: 1)
    print(p.y)
}
```

## Fixed

```sisu
class Point {
    x: i64
}

fn main() {
    let p = Point(x: 1)
    print(p.x)
}
```
